<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout component="day15" xml:id="day-15-lecture">
    <title>Day 15</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            Ch 5.1.5: <m>k</m>-fold Cross-Validation for Classification
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <objectives>
        <title>Covered in this class</title>

        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    CV for classification
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Best subet selection
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>CV for Classification</title>

        <p>
            Setup: LOOCV
        </p>

        <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
            <ul>
                <li>
                    Remove the <m>i</m> th point <m>(x_i,y_i)</m> and reserve it
                    for testing.
                </li>

                <li>Train the model on the remaining points.</li>
                <li>
                    Calculate
                    <m>\operatorname{Err}_i = \mathbb{I}(y_i \ne \widehat{y}_i)</m>.
                </li>

                <li>Rinse and repeat.</li>
            </ul>

            <stack>
                <image source="figures/LOOCV-subset0.png" xml:id="loocv-subset-0">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Leave-one-out cross-validation split with the first
                        point reserved for testing.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

                <image source="figures/LOOCV-subset1.png" xml:id="loocv-subset-1">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Leave-one-out cross-validation split with the second
                        point reserved for testing.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

                <image source="figures/LOOCV-subset2.png" xml:id="loocv-subset-2">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Leave-one-out cross-validation split with the third
                        point reserved for testing.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

                <image source="figures/LOOCV-subset3.png" xml:id="loocv-subset-3">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Leave-one-out cross-validation split with the fourth
                        point reserved for testing.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

                <image source="figures/LOOCV-subset4.png" xml:id="loocv-subset-4">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Leave-one-out cross-validation split with the fifth
                        point reserved for testing.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

                <p>
                    <m>\vdots</m>
                </p>

                <image source="figures/LOOCV-subset29.png" xml:id="loocv-subset-29">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Leave-one-out cross-validation split with the final
                        point reserved for testing.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </stack>
        </sidebyside>

        <p>
            Return the cross-validation error:
            <md>
                CV_{(n)} = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\operatorname{Err}_i
            </md>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>CV for Classification</title>

        <p>
            Setup: <m>k</m>-fold
        </p>

        <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
            <ul>
                <li>Randomly split the data into <m>k</m> groups (folds).</li>
                <li>
                    Make the folds approximately equal in size.
                    For notation, suppose each fold has <m>\ell</m> points.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Remove the <m>i</m> th fold <m>U_i</m> and reserve it for
                    testing.
                </li>

                <li>Train the model on the remaining points.</li>
                <li>
                    Calculate
                    <md>
                        \operatorname{Err}_i = \frac{1}{\ell}
        \sum_{(x_j,y_j)\in U_i}
        \mathbb{I}(y_j \ne \widehat{y}_j)
                    </md>
                </li>

                <li>Rinse and repeat.</li>
            </ul>

            <stack>
                <image source="figures/CV-Kfold-subset0.png" xml:id="cv-kfold-subset-0">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Data split into folds, with the first fold reserved for
                        testing.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

                <image source="figures/CV-Kfold-subset1.png" xml:id="cv-kfold-subset-1">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Data split into folds, with the second fold reserved for
                        testing.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

                <image source="figures/CV-Kfold-subset2.png" xml:id="cv-kfold-subset-2">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Data split into folds, with the third fold reserved for
                        testing.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

                <image source="figures/CV-Kfold-subset3.png" xml:id="cv-kfold-subset-3">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Data split into folds, with the fourth fold reserved for
                        testing.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

                <image source="figures/CV-Kfold-subset4.png" xml:id="cv-kfold-subset-4">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Data split into folds, with the fifth fold reserved for
                        testing.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </stack>
        </sidebyside>

        <p>
            Return the cross-validation error:
            <md>
                CV_{(k)} = \frac{1}{k}\sum_{i=1}^{k}\operatorname{Err}_i
            </md>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Classification on simulated data: logistic regression</title>

        <p>
            No <m>k</m>-fold cross-validation yet.
        </p>

        <sidebyside widths="40% 45%">
            <image source="figures/Fig5-7a.png" xml:id="fig5-7a">
                <shortdescription>
                    Plot comparing the Bayes decision boundary in purple with
                    the logistic regression boundary in black.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>

            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Purple: Bayes decision boundary.
                            <ul>
                                <li>Error rate: <m>0.133</m></li>
                            </ul>
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Black: logistic regression.
                            <ul>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>\log\left(\frac{p}{1-p}\right) = \beta_0 + \beta_1 X_1 + \beta_2 X_2</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Error rate: <m>0.201</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        This error rate is still high, so the
                                        model has not done a great job yet.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ul>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </sidebyside>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>Increase the degree and see what happens.</li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <note>
        <p>
            The Bayes decision boundary is where <m>p = 1-p</m>, meaning
            <m>\beta_0 + \beta_1 X_1 + \beta_2 X_2 = 0</m>
        </p>
    </note>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>
            Classification on simulated data: Quadratic logistic regression
        </title>

        <p>
            No <m>k</m>-fold cross-validation yet.
        </p>

        <sidebyside widths="40% 45%">
            <image source="figures/Fig5-7b.png" xml:id="fig5-7b">
                <shortdescription>
                    Plot comparing the Bayes decision boundary in purple with
                    the quadratic logistic regression boundary in black.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>

            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Purple: Bayes decision boundary.
                            <ul>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Error rate: <m>0.133</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ul>
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Black: logistic regression.
                            <ul>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>\log\left(\frac{p}{1-p}\right) = \beta_0 + \beta_1 X_1 + \beta_2 X_1^2 + \beta_3 X_2 + \beta_4 X_2^2</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Error rate: <m>0.197</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        The logistic regression error rate
                                        improved slightly, but is still not
                                        great.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ul>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </sidebyside>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Increase the degree again and see what happens.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Example on simulated data: Polynomial Logistic Regression</title>

        <p>
            No <m>k</m>-fold cross-validation yet.
        </p>

        <sidebyside widths="45% 35%">
            <image source="figures/Fig5-7.png" xml:id="fig5-7">
                <shortdescription>
                    Simulated data with the Bayes decision boundary and logistic
                    regression decision boundaries for polynomial degrees one
                    through four.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>

            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Purple: Bayes decision boundary.
                            <ul>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Error rate: <m>0.133</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ul>
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Black: logistic regression.
                            <ul>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Degree 1 error rate: <m>0.201</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Degree 2 error rate: <m>0.197</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Degree 3 error rate: <m>0.160</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Degree 4 error rate: <m>0.162</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ul>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </sidebyside>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Normally, we do not have the Bayes decision boundary.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        How do you pick between models?
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Decide degree based on CV</title>

        <sidebyside widths="45% 50%">
            <image source="figures/Fig5-8a.png" xml:id="fig5-8a">
                <shortdescription>
                    Plot of test error, training error, and ten-fold
                    cross-validation error as model flexibility increases.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>

            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Test error (brown)
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Training error (blue)
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            10-fold CV error (black)
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </sidebyside>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Training error tends to decrease as the flexibility of
                        the fit increases.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        The blue curve does not decrease monotonically, but it
                        trends downward overall.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Test error displays a characteristic U-shape.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        The 10-fold CV error provides a fairly good, but
                        underestimated, approximation to the test error rate.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        The minimum occurs at degree 4, close to the test error
                        curve's minimum at degree 3.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Similar approach for KNN</title>

        <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
            <image source="figures/Fig5-8b.png" xml:id="fig5-8b">
                <shortdescription>
                    Plot of test error, training error, and ten-fold
                    cross-validation error for K-nearest neighbors across values
                    of 1/K.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>

            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Test error (brown)
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Training error (blue)
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            10-fold CV error (black)
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </sidebyside>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Here, the changing parameter is different.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        The same idea helps identify a suitable choice of
                        <m>K</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        The minimum of the black curve (10-fold CV) occurs at a
                        value of <m>1/K</m> close to the minimum of the brown
                        curve, suggesting a good choice.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>TL;DR</title>

        <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
            <stack>
                <p>
                    <m>k</m>-fold CV
                </p>

                <image source="figures/CV-Kfold-subset0.png">
                    <shortdescription>
                        First split in the k-fold cross-validation sequence.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

                <image source="figures/CV-Kfold-subset1.png">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Second split in the k-fold cross-validation sequence.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

                <image source="figures/CV-Kfold-subset2.png">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Third split in the k-fold cross-validation sequence.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

                <image source="figures/CV-Kfold-subset3.png">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Fourth split in the k-fold cross-validation sequence.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

                <image source="figures/CV-Kfold-subset4.png">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Fifth split in the k-fold cross-validation sequence.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

                <p>
                    <md>
                        CV_{(k)} = \frac{1}{k}\sum_{i=1}^{k}\operatorname{MSE}_i
                    </md>
                </p>

                <p>
                    Usually, use <m>k=5</m> or <m>k=10</m>.
                </p>
            </stack>

            <stack>
                <p>
                    <m>k</m>-fold CV for classification
                </p>

                <p>
                    <md>
                        \operatorname{Err}_i = \mathbb{I}(y_j \ne \widehat{y}_j)
                    </md>
                </p>

                <p>
                    <md>
                        CV_{(k)} = \frac{1}{k}\sum_{i=1}^{k}\operatorname{Err}_i
                    </md>
                </p>
            </stack>
        </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Improving linear models:Subset selection</title>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Why shouldn't you have as many predictors in your model
                        as possible?
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        What are the three basic methods for selecting
                        appropriate predictors (feature selection)?
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        What are the steps in the algorithm for each of these
                        methods?
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        How can you carry out these procedures by hand, given
                        the training and cross-validation test errors for each
                        combination of predictors?
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        What are the pros and cons of each feature selection
                        method?
                        When can or can't you use them?
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Subset selection</title>

        <p>
            The problem of many features (p) relative to samples (n)
        </p>

        <p>
            Up to now, we've focused on the standard linear model:
            <md>
                Y = \beta_0 + \beta_1 X_1 + \cdots + \beta_p X_p + \varepsilon
            </md>
            and done least squares estimation.
        </p>

        <p>
            Translation: minimize <m>RSS = \sum_i (y_i - \hat{y}_i)^2</m>.
        </p>

        <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
            <stack>
                <p>
                    Prediction accuracy
                </p>

                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                If the true relationship is approximately
                                linear, least squares estimates have low bias.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                If <m>n \gg p</m>, least squares also has low
                                variance.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                If <m>n</m> is not much larger than <m>p</m>,
                                estimates have high variability, which can lead
                                to overfitting and poor predictions.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                If <m>n &lt; p</m>, there is no unique solution,
                                so least squares cannot be used.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Goal for the next slide: shrink or constrain the
                                number of variables to improve accuracy.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </stack>

            <stack>
                <p>
                    Model interpretability
                </p>

                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Often, many included variables are not
                                associated with the response.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Including them leads to unnecessary complexity
                                in the model.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Idea: set these coefficients to zero or close to
                                zero.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Goal for the next slide: use automatic feature
                                selection (variable selection) to remove
                                unnecessary variables.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </stack>
        </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Best subset selection</title>

        <p>
            Go through every combination of variables exhaustively.
        </p>

        <p>
            All subsets of 4 variables ( <m>2^4 = 16</m> ):
        </p>

        <sidebyside widths="5% 10% 10% 15% 15%">
            <stack>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\emptyset</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </stack>

            <stack>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>X_1</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>X_2</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>X_3</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>X_4</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </stack>

            <stack>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>X_1, X_2</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>X_1, X_3</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>X_1, X_4</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>X_2, X_3</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>X_2, X_4</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>X_3, X_4</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </stack>

            <stack>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>X_1, X_2, X_3</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>X_1, X_2, X_4</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>X_1, X_3, X_4</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>X_2, X_3, X_4</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </stack>

            <stack>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>X_1, X_2, X_3, X_4</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </stack>
        </sidebyside>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The idea is to fit the model <m>2^p</m> times, score
                        each model, and choose the one with the best score.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        The subsets are grouped into tiers by the number of
                        variables <m>k</m> selected from the <m>p</m> available
                        variables.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Models with the same <m>k</m> can be compared without
                        considering the order of the variables.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Each tier has <m>\binom{p}{k}</m> combinations, where
                        <md>
                            \binom{p}{k} = \dfrac{p!}{k!(p-k)!}
                        </md>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>One way of breaking this up</title>

        <algorithm xml:id="best-subset-selection">
            <title>Best subset selection</title>

            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Let <m>\mathcal{M}_0</m> denote the
                            <term>null model</term>, which contains no
                            predictors.
                            This model simply predicts the sample mean for each
                            observation.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            For <m>k = 1, 2, \ldots, p</m>:
                            <ol marker="(a)">
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Fit all <m>\binom{p}{k}</m> models that
                                        contain exactly <m>k</m> predictors.
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Pick the best among these
                                        <m>\binom{p}{k}</m> models, and call it
                                        <m>\mathcal{M}_k</m>.
                                        Here, <term>best</term> is defined as
                                        having the smallest RSS, or
                                        equivalently, the largest <m>R^2</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ol>
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Select a single best model from among
                            <m>\mathcal{M}_0, \ldots, \mathcal{M}_p</m> using
                            cross-validated prediction error, <m>C_p</m> (AIC),
                            BIC, or adjusted <m>R^2</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </algorithm>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Part 2b chooses the model with the lowest training
                        score; Part 3 then chooses the model with the lowest
                        testing score.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Step 2 is computationally infeasible for large <m>p</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>

        <example>
            <title>Best subset selection</title>

            <introduction>
                <p>
                    We train a model using four variables,
                    <m>X_1, X_2, X_3, X_4</m>.
                    We’re interested in selecting a subset of the variables to
                    use.
                    The following table shows the mean squared error (MSE) for
                    the model learned using each possible subset of variables.
                </p>

                <table>
                    <title>
                        Training MSE and k-fold Cross-Validation Testing Error
                    </title>

                    <tabular>
                        <row header="yes">
                            <cell>Model</cell>
                            <cell>Training MSE ( <m>\times 10^7</m> )</cell>
                            <cell>k-fold CV Testing Error</cell>
                        </row>

                        <row>
                            <cell>Null model</cell>
                            <cell>8.76</cell>
                            <cell>10.08</cell>
                        </row>

                        <row>
